---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Research Methodology"
  description="How Invest Gulf researches UAE property data, sources, limitations, and partner referral policy."
>
  <section class="site-container py-12 md:py-16 max-w-3xl">
    <h1 class="mb-6">Methodology</h1>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Our guides combine publicly available regulatory sources (DLD, RERA, ICA visa rules), market reports,
        and cross-checks against multiple listing platforms. Figures are rounded and dated, markets move quickly.
      </p>
      <h2>Sources we prioritise</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Official UAE government and emirate-level portals</li>
        <li>Licensed broker market commentary (clearly labelled as third-party)</li>
        <li>Transaction fee schedules published by land departments</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Lead referral policy</h2>
      <p>
        When you submit the shortlist form, your enquiry may be shared with a <strong>RERA-licensed partner</strong>
        who can show regulated inventory and complete KYC. We do not sell your data to unrelated marketing lists.
      </p>
      <h2>How we treat rents and yields</h2>
      <p>
        Rent figures are drawn from registered tenancy contracts wherever the registry publishes them: Ejari in Dubai,
        Tawtheeq in Abu Dhabi, and the equivalent record in other Gulf markets. Portal asking rents typically sit 10% to 15%
        above achieved rents in competitive buildings, so we do not use them as an income base.
      </p>
      <p>
        Yields are quoted net rather than gross wherever we have the inputs to calculate one. Net means after building-level
        service charges, management at 5% to 8% for long-term lets, a vacancy allowance matched to the market rather than a
        flat assumption, and against total acquisition cost including transfer fees rather than headline purchase price.
        Where only a gross figure is available, we label it as gross.
      </p>

      <h2>What we do not do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>We do not accept payment from developers for coverage, placement or ranking in any guide or shortlist.</li>
        <li>We do not publish projected yields supplied by a developer without labelling them as projections.</li>
        <li>We do not present another firm's research or underwriting as our own.</li>
        <li>We are not a licensed broker, a developer, or a listing portal, and we do not hold client funds.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Limitations</h2>
      <p>
        Gulf property data is uneven. Dubai and Abu Dhabi publish enough transaction and tenancy data to support building-level
        analysis; Saudi designated zones, Omani ITC areas and parts of the Qatari market do not yet. Where the evidence is thin
        we say so rather than filling the gap with a market average, and we treat early-stage markets as early-stage rather than
        pricing them against mature comparables.
      </p>
      <p>
        Regulatory positions change, sometimes between a page being written and read. Thresholds, fees and eligibility rules on
        this site should be confirmed with the relevant authority before any transaction.
      </p>

      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        If a figure on this site is wrong, we would rather know. Email <a href="mailto:info@invest-gulf.com">info@invest-gulf.com</a>
        with the page and the source. Material corrections are made to the page and reflected in its updated date.
      </p>

      <h2>Updates</h2>
      <p>
        Each guide shows an <em>Updated</em> date when material facts change (visa thresholds, fee changes, major market shifts).
        Always confirm numbers with your lawyer and broker before signing.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
